+# Agent Note: Community Windows updates through GitHub Releases
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-11-13-community-windows-github-updates.zh.md)
+
+This downstream decision is an explicit exception to the official [signed Desktop release policy](../architecture/2026-08-25-electron-desktop-packaging-and-updates.md).
+
+## Problem
+
+The community Windows package has no DeepSeek signing certificate or access to the official COS release service. Omitting updater metadata forces every user to discover, download, and install each community preview manually, but treating an unsigned package as an official release would discard the publisher guarantee that the standard Desktop release requires.
+
+## Decision
+
+An unsigned Windows x64 package can opt into a downstream generic-provider URL through `DSH_DESKTOP_COMMUNITY_UPDATE_URL` in `.env.windows`. The setting is rejected for signed packages, other platforms, other architectures, inherited process configuration, non-HTTPS URLs, credentials, queries, and fragments. Ordinary unsigned packages continue to omit updater metadata.
+
+The community package uses the `nightly` channel and omits the mandatory DeepSeek update-policy service. GitHub Actions uploads the versioned NSIS installer before replacing the fixed feed metadata. The application checks in the background, but `electron-updater` does not download or install until the user confirms each operation.
+
+The feed's SHA-512 value detects a corrupt or mismatched installer. It does not authenticate the publisher because the installer and feed share the same GitHub repository trust. Windows therefore continues to identify the package as an unknown publisher until the community release process gains a code-signing certificate.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Reuse the official COS configuration.** The community repository has neither the official bucket credentials nor authority to publish under the DeepSeek download origin.
+
+**Enable updater metadata for every unsigned package.** Local diagnostic packages must remain inert and cannot inherit a release destination accidentally. A file-owned opt-in keeps the exception narrow.
+
+**Mark the community package as signed without a certificate.** Disabling or imitating publisher verification would present an assurance the package does not provide. The package remains visibly unsigned and documents the limitation.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Users manually install one bootstrap community version and can then receive later community previews through the in-app confirmation flow. A compromised repository or release account can replace both metadata and installer, so SHA-512 is not a substitute for Authenticode. Official signed builds, upload qualification, COS feeds, and release records remain unchanged.
